code_review.final_verdict:
VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED

The structure is largely idiomatic and additive — the new `DuplicateTelephoneException` sits in `rest.advice` alongside `InvalidOwnerFieldsException`, the exception handler faithfully mirrors the existing handlers, and extracting a `requireUniqueTelephone` helper keeps `addOwner` readable. One concrete issue:

**Duplicated normalization logic in `requireUniqueTelephone` (OwnerRestControllerV1).** The same class already owns `normalizeTelephone(...)`, and `addOwner` calls it one line before invoking this helper. But `requireUniqueTelephone` re-implements the digit-stripping inline with `existing.replaceAll("\\D", "")` rather than reusing `normalizeTelephone`. This forks the definition of "normalized telephone" into two places within a single class — if the normalization rule ever changes (e.g. stripping a leading country code), one site will drift from the other, and the uniqueness check would silently diverge from what `addOwner` actually stores. Normalize the existing owner's value through the same `normalizeTelephone(existing)` call so there is one authoritative definition:

```java
private void requireUniqueTelephone(String telephone) {
    boolean inUse = this.clinicService.findAllOwners().stream()
        .map(Owner::getTelephone)
        .filter(existing -> existing != null)
        .map(this::normalizeTelephone)
        .anyMatch(telephone::equals);
    if (inUse) {
        throw new DuplicateTelephoneException(telephone);
    }
}
```

(This keeps the fix local to the helper; the `null` guard is still useful since stored legacy values may be null.)

